  "worked_solution": "We consider the cases $\\lambda =0$ and $\\lambda \\ne 0$ separately, since they correspond to different solutions. \n\n**Case 1**: First, we consider $\\lambda =0.$ \n\nSetting $\\lambda =0$ gives the equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2033}=0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation gives\n\n$$\ny=Ax+B.\n$$\n\nApplying the conditions ${y}^{\u2032}(0)={y}^{\u2032}(\\pi )=0$ yields $A=0.$ Thus, we have a nontrivial solution, so $\\lambda =0$ is a parameter value corresponding to the eigenvalue.\n\n**Case 2**: Next, we consider $\\lambda >0.$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}+16{\\lambda }^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have $m=\\pm 4\\text{i}\\lambda .$ So, the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4\\lambda x),\\,{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4\\lambda x)\n$$\n\nand the general solution is\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4\\lambda x)+B\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}=-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4\\lambda x)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{\u2032}(0)=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(0)+4B\\lambda \\operatorname{cos}\u2061(0)=0\n$$\n\nwhich gives $B=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\ny=A\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4\\lambda x).\n$$\n\nApplying the boundary condition ${y}^{\u2032}(\\pi )=0$ yields\n\n$$\n-4A\\lambda \\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4\\lambda \\pi )=0.\n$$\n\nFor nontrivial solutions, we require \n\n$$\n4\\lambda \\pi =n\\pi \n$$\n\nfor $n\\in N.$ This gives the parameter values corresponding to the eigenvalues:\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{n}=\\frac{n}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe corresponding eigenfunctions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{n}(x)={B}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4{\\lambda }_{n}x),\\,{B}_{n}\\in R.\n$$",
